 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Myrtle Beach golf community membership is optional almost everywhere</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Grand Strand has more than 80 championship courses, and most golf-community homes here are on public courses. Arrowhead, Wild Wing, Blackmoor, Legends, International Club, Wachesaw East, and TPC Myrtle Beach all sell tee times to anyone, so living there requires no membership at all. Where clubs exist, membership is a choice: Barefoot, Prestwick, Pawleys Plantation, and Grande Dunes all separate homeownership from club dues.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">The two real exceptions run in opposite directions. Wachesaw Plantation is private and member-owned, and while you can own without joining, every amenity belongs to the club, so a non-member owns a house behind a gate and nothing else. The Dunes Club is private, with limited package access, and its surrounding streets are some of the only prestige addresses here with no mandatory HOA. Details like those matter more than the name of the course designer when you are picking a community.</p></div></section>
 
